{"journal_version":1,"sequence":5,"replace_from":2,"message_count":3,"messages":[{"role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"We need answer review. Must first line exactly VERDICT: PASS or FAIL. Need inspect diff for concrete regressions. We have no tools. Need reason carefully.\n\nTask AsyncPanel. Acceptance:\n- success path at / unchanged never retry.\n- /?sync=error error state button named Retry sync.\n- Activating immediately returns panel to Checking service… then completes All changes synced after same delay.\n- retry disappears after success and cannot create overlapping timers repeated activation.\n- retry must not reload page or reset selected task.\n- preserve all existing behavior, build/tests green.\n\nDiff: mount effect reads requested and schedule. schedule sets timerRef. return clearTimer. But eslint comment before deps? It says:\nuseEffect(() => {\n ...\n return clearTimer;\n // Runs once...\n // eslint-disable...\n}, []);\nNo issue.\n\nRetry handler: if timerRef.current !== undefined return; setState('loading'); schedule('success'). Immediately returns loading. Good. Button disappears on success. Overlap prevented by guard but subtle: React state set loading is batched; rapid repeated click before re-render? TimerRef set synchronously by schedule after setState, so subsequent click timer pending -> return. Good.\n\nPotential issue: mount effect cleanup clearTimer: on unmount. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: effect closure captures schedule and clearTimer defined each render but effect [] runs initial. clearTimer uses ref. fine.\n\nPotential issue: On retry, button remains? setState loading causes re-render, error block removed. Good. Loading text? Need inspect existing render not fully in diff. We need know conditional display. Likely state === 'loading' shows Checking service…, success shows All changes synced., error shows alert. Diff only shows error part. Retry adds button.\n\nPotential issue: aria-live section. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: `timerRef` type `useRef<number | undefined>(undefined)` but in browser window.setTimeout returns number. fine.\n\nPotential issue: `clearTimer` not stable, effect dependency disabled. okay.\n\nPotential regression: Original mount effect did `const requested = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('sync'); setState(requested === 'error' ? 'error' : 'success');` after 120. New same. But in new mount effect, it calls schedule which uses SYNC_DELAY. same 120. Good.\n\nPotential issue: initial retry action appears only error. success never. If URL has ?sync=error, mount initially loading, then error. Button. If initial path `/`, loading then success. unchanged. If URL ?sync=error and user retries: state loading, then success. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"cannot create overlapping timers from repeated activation\" The guard checks timerRef. Is there any window where timerRef undefined while a retry timer pending? schedule sets timerRef before. Initial effect also sets timerRef. On retry when state error, the initial timer has already fired and set timerRef undefined. Good. Repeated activation after success impossible because button gone. Repeated activation during loading impossible because button gone. If activated multiple times synchronously before React rerender: first retry calls setState loading schedule sets ref; second retry sees ref -> return. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Keyboard/focus: when button activated via keyboard Enter, state loading removes button, focus moves to body maybe. Acceptance not mention focus. Accessibility maybe okay. No requirement.\n\nPotential issue: clicking retry triggers no navigation because type button. Good. Selected task outside component unaffected, but does parent rerender? The component state updates, parent? Only component. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: \"immediately returns panel to Checking service…\" The setState('loading') happens but React may not commit until after handler returns; acceptable immediate. If error state also displays text and button, when loading state updates. Good.\n\nPotential issue: No public test for retry, but not required. We should not fail solely lacking test.\n\nNeed maybe inspect styles: `.sync-state.error { display: grid; gap: 4px; ... }` button secondary styles. `.sync-retry { justify-self: start; margin-top: 8px; }`. Good. Existing focus-visible outline? report claims. No issue.\n\nCould there be a regression from moving timer logic: original effect cleanup cleared timer. New effect calls `schedule` then returns `clearTimer`. But `clearTimer` is redefined every render, but effect captures initial clearTimer. Ref shared. Fine. If component state changes before timeout (could only via retry? no button while loading), effect cleanup not run until unmount. Fine.\n\nImportant subtle bug: In useEffect, they call `schedule` but `schedule` sets `timerRef.current`; then return `clearTimer`. On React StrictMode dev, effect mounts, schedules, cleanup clears, effect re-mounts? In React 18 strict mode dev, effects run setup, cleanup, setup. If schedule on first setup then cleanup clearTimer; second setup schedule. Fine. Original had timer variable. No issue. But if strict mode cleanup after retry? effect cleanup only on unmount or strict double-invoke. Not.\n\nPotential issue with `clearTimer` in cleanup: If timer already fired, timerRef.current undefined. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: The guard `if (timerRef.current !== undefined) return;` also prevents retry if initial loading timer pending, but button not present. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: The initial effect ignores dependency `schedule` with eslint disable. Build may treat lint? npm run build tsc+vite no lint. Fine. But eslint comment placed immediately before `}, []);`? Let's parse:\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const requested = ...\n    schedule(...);\n    return clearTimer;\n    // Runs once on mount; retries are scheduled from the click handler instead.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, []);\n\nThe eslint-disable-next-line is on the line before `}, []);`, so it disables exhaustive-deps for dependency array line. That's okay. But comments after return statement are unreachable? In JS, comments don't matter. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: \"Retry action disappears after success and cannot create overlapping timers from repeated activation.\" Maybe guard only prevents overlapping if timerRef current is set; however after timer fires, it sets timerRef undefined then setState('success'). If user clicks at exact moment after timer fires but before React commits success, button still in DOM and timerRef undefined. Can a click occur in that tiny window? JS single-threaded: timer callback runs, sets ref undefined and calls setState; React may batch state update until end of timer. User click event cannot interleave before timer callback completes and before React flush? React may flush sync? In React 18, setState in timeout is batched and flushed after callback, likely before next event. But even if click event queued, it won't execute until timer callback returns. If React doesn't commit before next click due concurrent? For discrete event, React flushes. Not a concrete concern. If guard isn't enough? Even if retry called after timer fired but before re-render, it would schedule another timer, but state success soon. Then timer later sets success again. Could create overlapping? Only one timer, not overlap. But could show loading? Actually retry sets loading and schedules success. Could briefly show loading after success? Edge. Not supported? Not concrete.\n\nMaybe requirement \"immediately returns panel to Checking service…\" The handler sets loading then schedules. But if repeated activation and timer pending, returns early leaving loading. fine.\n\nCould there be a regression: The component no longer has trailing newline? Diff removes blank line? Not relevant.\n\nNeed maybe consider public tests might inspect exact text. Existing success path unchanged. Error text unchanged plus button. Fine.\n\nNo concrete fail. Need output PASS. But we need be thorough? Desired final concise. We can mention no findings. The instruction: first line exactly VERDICT: PASS. Then maybe summary. We can provide PASS with maybe no findings. Need not overdo. Ensure first line exact.\n\nBut let's double-check if there is any acceptance unmet around \"same simulated delay\". Retry uses SYNC_DELAY 120. good.\n\n\"Activating it immediately returns the panel to `Checking service…`\" Does existing render display \"Checking service…\" for loading? We didn't see. Diff only shows error block. Assume existing. But could loading state also show error? Need infer. The error block is conditional. There are three states. likely:\n{state === 'loading' && <p className=\"sync-state loading\">Checking service…</p>}\n{state === 'success' && <p className=\"sync-state success\">All changes are synced.</p>}\n{state === 'error' && (...)}\nSo yes.\n\n\"never shows retry action\" on success. The button is inside `state === 'error'`. yes.\n\n\"cannot create overlapping timers from repeated activation\" Guard. yes.\n\nNo page reload. type button. no location. selected task outside. yes.\n\nCould unmount cleanup preserve original? Original cleanup per effect. New clearTimer shared. If unmount while retry timer pending, effect cleanup runs? The effect cleanup is registered from mount and runs on unmount; it calls clearTimer, clearing current timerRef (including retry timer). Good. Actually yes, effect cleanup on unmount. Preserves and handles retry. If the effect cleanup closure's clearTimer uses ref, yes. Good.\n\nOne subtle: The effect's cleanup does not run when component state changes, only unmount. So it clears retry timer on unmount. Good.\n\nThus PASS.\n\nNeed final only. Could say \"VERDICT: PASS\" and brief \"No concrete regression...\" Since first line exactly. Fine.","content":"VERDICT: PASS\n\nNo concrete regression or unmet acceptance criterion found in the supplied diff. The retry path sets `loading` immediately, schedules the same 120 ms transition, guards against overlapping timers via `timerRef`, renders only in the `error` state, and does not touch navigation or external selected-task state.","response_items":[{"id":"9e2bc703-9e9c-443e-8b74-34e9a55b91b6","summary":[],"type":"reasoning","content":[{"text":"We need answer review. Must first line exactly VERDICT: PASS or FAIL. Need inspect diff for concrete regressions. We have no tools. Need reason carefully.\n\nTask AsyncPanel. Acceptance:\n- success path at / unchanged never retry.\n- /?sync=error error state button named Retry sync.\n- Activating immediately returns panel to Checking service… then completes All changes synced after same delay.\n- retry disappears after success and cannot create overlapping timers repeated activation.\n- retry must not reload page or reset selected task.\n- preserve all existing behavior, build/tests green.\n\nDiff: mount effect reads requested and schedule. schedule sets timerRef. return clearTimer. But eslint comment before deps? It says:\nuseEffect(() => {\n ...\n return clearTimer;\n // Runs once...\n // eslint-disable...\n}, []);\nNo issue.\n\nRetry handler: if timerRef.current !== undefined return; setState('loading'); schedule('success'). Immediately returns loading. Good. Button disappears on success. Overlap prevented by guard but subtle: React state set loading is batched; rapid repeated click before re-render? TimerRef set synchronously by schedule after setState, so subsequent click timer pending -> return. Good.\n\nPotential issue: mount effect cleanup clearTimer: on unmount. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: effect closure captures schedule and clearTimer defined each render but effect [] runs initial. clearTimer uses ref. fine.\n\nPotential issue: On retry, button remains? setState loading causes re-render, error block removed. Good. Loading text? Need inspect existing render not fully in diff. We need know conditional display. Likely state === 'loading' shows Checking service…, success shows All changes synced., error shows alert. Diff only shows error part. Retry adds button.\n\nPotential issue: aria-live section. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: `timerRef` type `useRef<number | undefined>(undefined)` but in browser window.setTimeout returns number. fine.\n\nPotential issue: `clearTimer` not stable, effect dependency disabled. okay.\n\nPotential regression: Original mount effect did `const requested = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get('sync'); setState(requested === 'error' ? 'error' : 'success');` after 120. New same. But in new mount effect, it calls schedule which uses SYNC_DELAY. same 120. Good.\n\nPotential issue: initial retry action appears only error. success never. If URL has ?sync=error, mount initially loading, then error. Button. If initial path `/`, loading then success. unchanged. If URL ?sync=error and user retries: state loading, then success. Good.\n\nPotential issue: \"cannot create overlapping timers from repeated activation\" The guard checks timerRef. Is there any window where timerRef undefined while a retry timer pending? schedule sets timerRef before. Initial effect also sets timerRef. On retry when state error, the initial timer has already fired and set timerRef undefined. Good. Repeated activation after success impossible because button gone. Repeated activation during loading impossible because button gone. If activated multiple times synchronously before React rerender: first retry calls setState loading schedule sets ref; second retry sees ref -> return. Good.\n\nPotential issue: Keyboard/focus: when button activated via keyboard Enter, state loading removes button, focus moves to body maybe. Acceptance not mention focus. Accessibility maybe okay. No requirement.\n\nPotential issue: clicking retry triggers no navigation because type button. Good. Selected task outside component unaffected, but does parent rerender? The component state updates, parent? Only component. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: \"immediately returns panel to Checking service…\" The setState('loading') happens but React may not commit until after handler returns; acceptable immediate. If error state also displays text and button, when loading state updates. Good.\n\nPotential issue: No public test for retry, but not required. We should not fail solely lacking test.\n\nNeed maybe inspect styles: `.sync-state.error { display: grid; gap: 4px; ... }` button secondary styles. `.sync-retry { justify-self: start; margin-top: 8px; }`. Good. Existing focus-visible outline? report claims. No issue.\n\nCould there be a regression from moving timer logic: original effect cleanup cleared timer. New effect calls `schedule` then returns `clearTimer`. But `clearTimer` is redefined every render, but effect captures initial clearTimer. Ref shared. Fine. If component state changes before timeout (could only via retry? no button while loading), effect cleanup not run until unmount. Fine.\n\nImportant subtle bug: In useEffect, they call `schedule` but `schedule` sets `timerRef.current`; then return `clearTimer`. On React StrictMode dev, effect mounts, schedules, cleanup clears, effect re-mounts? In React 18 strict mode dev, effects run setup, cleanup, setup. If schedule on first setup then cleanup clearTimer; second setup schedule. Fine. Original had timer variable. No issue. But if strict mode cleanup after retry? effect cleanup only on unmount or strict double-invoke. Not.\n\nPotential issue with `clearTimer` in cleanup: If timer already fired, timerRef.current undefined. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: The guard `if (timerRef.current !== undefined) return;` also prevents retry if initial loading timer pending, but button not present. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: The initial effect ignores dependency `schedule` with eslint disable. Build may treat lint? npm run build tsc+vite no lint. Fine. But eslint comment placed immediately before `}, []);`? Let's parse:\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const requested = ...\n    schedule(...);\n    return clearTimer;\n    // Runs once on mount; retries are scheduled from the click handler instead.\n    // eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps\n  }, []);\n\nThe eslint-disable-next-line is on the line before `}, []);`, so it disables exhaustive-deps for dependency array line. That's okay. But comments after return statement are unreachable? In JS, comments don't matter. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: \"Retry action disappears after success and cannot create overlapping timers from repeated activation.\" Maybe guard only prevents overlapping if timerRef current is set; however after timer fires, it sets timerRef undefined then setState('success'). If user clicks at exact moment after timer fires but before React commits success, button still in DOM and timerRef undefined. Can a click occur in that tiny window? JS single-threaded: timer callback runs, sets ref undefined and calls setState; React may batch state update until end of timer. User click event cannot interleave before timer callback completes and before React flush? React may flush sync? In React 18, setState in timeout is batched and flushed after callback, likely before next event. But even if click event queued, it won't execute until timer callback returns. If React doesn't commit before next click due concurrent? For discrete event, React flushes. Not a concrete concern. If guard isn't enough? Even if retry called after timer fired but before re-render, it would schedule another timer, but state success soon. Then timer later sets success again. Could create overlapping? Only one timer, not overlap. But could show loading? Actually retry sets loading and schedules success. Could briefly show loading after success? Edge. Not supported? Not concrete.\n\nMaybe requirement \"immediately returns panel to Checking service…\" The handler sets loading then schedules. But if repeated activation and timer pending, returns early leaving loading. fine.\n\nCould there be a regression: The component no longer has trailing newline? Diff removes blank line? Not relevant.\n\nNeed maybe consider public tests might inspect exact text. Existing success path unchanged. Error text unchanged plus button. Fine.\n\nNo concrete fail. Need output PASS. But we need be thorough? Desired final concise. We can mention no findings. The instruction: first line exactly VERDICT: PASS. Then maybe summary. We can provide PASS with maybe no findings. Need not overdo. Ensure first line exact.\n\nBut let's double-check if there is any acceptance unmet around \"same simulated delay\". Retry uses SYNC_DELAY 120. good.\n\n\"Activating it immediately returns the panel to `Checking service…`\" Does existing render display \"Checking service…\" for loading? We didn't see. Diff only shows error block. Assume existing. But could loading state also show error? Need infer. The error block is conditional. There are three states. likely:\n{state === 'loading' && <p className=\"sync-state loading\">Checking service…</p>}\n{state === 'success' && <p className=\"sync-state success\">All changes are synced.</p>}\n{state === 'error' && (...)}\nSo yes.\n\n\"never shows retry action\" on success. The button is inside `state === 'error'`. yes.\n\n\"cannot create overlapping timers from repeated activation\" Guard. yes.\n\nNo page reload. type button. no location. selected task outside. yes.\n\nCould unmount cleanup preserve original? Original cleanup per effect. New clearTimer shared. If unmount while retry timer pending, effect cleanup runs? The effect cleanup is registered from mount and runs on unmount; it calls clearTimer, clearing current timerRef (including retry timer). Good. Actually yes, effect cleanup on unmount. Preserves and handles retry. If the effect cleanup closure's clearTimer uses ref, yes. Good.\n\nOne subtle: The effect's cleanup does not run when component state changes, only unmount. So it clears retry timer on unmount. Good.\n\nThus PASS.\n\nNeed final only. Could say \"VERDICT: PASS\" and brief \"No concrete regression...\" Since first line exactly. Fine.","type":"reasoning_text"}],"encrypted_content":"c770be53-057f-4f85-9901-6b50815a364b-0","status":"completed"},{"id":"f3e8302f-0aa6-4c68-bf5e-8d6f90814df1","content":[{"annotations":[],"text":"VERDICT: PASS\n\nNo concrete regression or unmet acceptance criterion found in the supplied diff. The retry path sets `loading` immediately, schedules the same 120 ms transition, guards against overlapping timers via `timerRef`, renders only in the `error` state, and does not touch navigation or external selected-task state.","type":"output_text","logprobs":[]}],"role":"assistant","status":"completed","type":"message","phase":"final_answer"}],"timestamp":"2026-11-22T15:50:35.566500+00:00"}],"meta":{"snapshot_version":1,"aid":1,"role":"workflow_agent","model":"deepseek-flash","session_state":{"used_tokens":3794,"context_tokens":1675,"step_count":1,"markup_recovered":0,"recent_call_hashes":[],"reads_since_last_edit":0,"has_landed_write":false,"low_yield_since_progress":0,"distinct_evidence_count":0,"scout_ledger":[],"steps_since_progress":0,"wind_down_done":false,"wind_down_attempts":0,"wind_down_token_mark":0,"budget_reserve_consumed":false,"loop_blocked_since_progress":0,"loop_state":{"version":1,"edit_seq":0,"blocked_rounds":0,"last_counted_step":-1,"operations":{}},"write_effect_unknown":false,"last_progress_unknown":false,"phase":"done","terminal_reason":"completed","pending_events":[],"pending_external_user_turn":null,"active_turn_start_message_index":null,"completed_turn_start_message_index":2,"pending_step_latency":null,"submitted_summary":null,"anthropic_history":null},"run_id":"workflow-f73e4e005dae4370bd22d1ad3710eeb3"},"seen_result_hashes_reset":false,"seen_result_hashes_added":[]}
